# shellcheck shell=bash
#
# shared/currency.lib -- does a BUILT artefact still describe this tree?
#
# SOURCED, never run: 0644 so `is_handler` (-f AND -x) cannot mistake it for a
# command. Requires `artefact.lib` sourced first and does NOT source it itself:
# the marker extraction site is that file, and a `source || define-my-own`
# fallback restores the duplicate at exactly the moment nobody is watching (vc).
#
# AND IT REQUIRES `sharedtarget.lib` SOURCED FIRST, ON THE SAME TERMS, SINCE
# 2026-09-05. Most of this file's consumers already sourced it; the ones that
# did not are the ones that had to change. It supplies two things this file spent
# its whole life re-deriving badly, and BOTH defects were the same shape -- the
# function agreeing with the truth by luck, with nothing distinguishing luck from
# construction:
#
#   1. THE SCOPE. `_rust_source_changed` diffed `-- native/rust` alone, while the
#      marker's base commit is `rev-list -1 HEAD` over SEVERAL pathspecs. So the
#      base was chosen over one population and the range measured over a narrower
#      one: a commit touching only `surface/` or `docs/design/` moved the build
#      inputs and this reported a source-clean range. NOT hypothetical -- recent
#      commits touching those paths touch no file under `native/rust`. It did
#      not fire on the day it was found because both scopes returned the same
#      count, which is exactly the condition under which nobody looks.
#   2. THE GIT ENVIRONMENT. Raw `git -C "$repo"` calls, in a check the
#      pre-commit gate RUNS, inside the environment where git exports `GIT_DIR`,
#      `GIT_INDEX_FILE` and `GIT_WORK_TREE`. `git -C` changes the directory and
#      overrides none of them, so the verdict was about whatever repository the
#      hook belonged to. `sharedtarget.lib` fixed this in itself on its first day
#      and wrote the paragraph explaining why; its sibling in the same directory,
#      sourced alongside it, never learned it. DRIVEN two-sided on two planted
#      repos that disagree without `GIT_DIR`: with `GIT_DIR` set the verdict
#      followed `GIT_DIR` in BOTH directions, and the dangerous direction is the
#      mirror -- a genuinely stale pair reported `ok`, which is the guard failing
#      OPEN, the one direction this file's own comments say an actor must never
#      fail.
#
# ONE LESSON FROM BOTH: a library that learns something does not teach its
# siblings, and `grep` for the fix is not the same as `grep` for the hazard.
#
# NOT `verify_pair` (shared/releasebuild.lib), AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE
# DESIGN. `verify_pair` is BUILD-time: on the shared path it demands the marker
# equal the build inputs' last commit exactly and treats any `dirty-` as bad.
# Right after `int local build`, WRONG at exec time, where it would refuse after
# any commit at all -- a README edit, a whiteboard fold -- which is how a gate
# becomes something people work around. TWO MECHANISMS ENFORCING DIFFERENT
# PROPERTIES ARE NOT TWO COPIES OF ONE (vc). The shared primitive is the pair
# read below; converging `verify_pair` onto it is RECORDED AND DELIBERATELY NOT
# DONE, so this header and `grep` still agree.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MATRIX KEYS ON DECIDABILITY, NEVER ON DIRT.
#
#   no marker at all                        REFUSE  "cannot say" is not a pass
#   the pair disagrees                      REFUSE  two trees, one install
#   base not reachable / not an ancestor    REFUSE  it describes a tree you are not on
#   clean  + range touches rust source      REFUSE  provably behind
#   dirty  + range touches rust source      REFUSE  same, and the dirt adds nothing
#   dirty  + range is source-clean          WARN    permanent, see below
#   clean  + range is source-clean          OK      silent
#
# THE CASE THAT LOOKS BACKWARDS is `clean + touches source` REFUSING while
# `dirty + source-clean` only WARNS. The matrix is not asking "is this binary
# tidy", it is asking "can I DECIDE whether it is behind".
#
# A DIRTY MARKER CAN NEVER REFUSE AND CAN NEVER BE CLEARED. `dirty-<base>` means
# built at <base> PLUS uncommitted modifications. The COMMITTED delta is
# decidable -- that is the range test. The UNCOMMITTED delta is not recoverable
# from the artefact by anyone, ever. So it cannot refuse (a dirty binary with a
# source-clean range may be perfectly current) and it cannot be discharged (no
# exec-time evidence establishes what the dirt was). A permanent uncleartable
# warning is unsatisfying and it is the only honest one: the alternative LOOKS
# clearable and quietly is not.
#
# THE BOUNDARY IS THE BUILD-INPUT SCOPE (`SHARED_TARGET_DIRT_SCOPES`, which
# includes all of `native/rust/`), NOT `native/rust/crates/`. A `Cargo.lock` or
# workspace `Cargo.toml` change is a different dependency set and therefore a
# different binary, and neither lives under `crates/`. Widening makes the guard
# refuse MORE, the correct direction when a false negative ships a stale binary
# and a false positive costs one rebuild.
#
# KNOWN FALSE REFUSAL, STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER: Rust puts unit tests
# INLINE under `#[cfg(test)]` in `src/*.rs`, so a commit touching only such a
# block is a source change by path and this refuses on it. Path is the only
# signal available without parsing Rust. Costs a rebuild, not a wrong answer.
#
# THE TWO "CANNOT SAY" CASES GO OPPOSITE WAYS. An ARTEFACT that cannot say what
# it is REFUSES. A TREE that cannot answer (no `git`) only WARNS -- that is a
# fact about the environment, not the artefact, and a wrapper that dies without
# `git` has invented a new dependency for every consumer.
#
# COST, DRIVEN AT `60782024` RATHER THAN ASSERTED: 40ms + 36ms for the two
# `strings` passes, 33ms for the git range, ~110ms total, against a 31ms floor
# for any check at all. It is stated because THE FIGURE IT REPLACES WAS ASSERTED
# AND WRONG BY TWO ORDERS OF MAGNITUDE -- `bin/intent3` carried "a MULTI-SECOND
# gate on every command" as the reason not to build this, and it held the design
# shut for three days.

# artefact_pair_marker <release_dir>
#
# Prints the ONE marker both binaries carry and returns 0, or prints a reason on
# stderr and returns 1. This is the primitive `verify_pair` also needs; it is
# here rather than there so the next spelling of "do the two agree" has a home to
# be added to instead of a place to be copied from.
artefact_pair_marker() {
  local dir="$1" b sc first="" first_bin=""
  for b in intent intentd; do
    if [ ! -f "$dir/$b" ]; then
      printf 'no %s binary at %s\n' "$b" "$dir/$b" >&2
      return 1
    fi
    if ! sc="$(artefact_source_commit "$dir/$b")"; then
      printf '%s carries NO source-commit marker -- it cannot say what it is\n' "$b" >&2
      return 1
    fi
    if [ -z "$first" ]; then
      first="$sc"
      first_bin="$b"
    elif [ "$sc" != "$first" ]; then
      printf 'the pair disagrees: %s says %s, %s says %s -- built from DIFFERENT trees\n' \
        "$first_bin" "$first" "$b" "$sc" >&2
      return 1
    fi
  done
  printf '%s' "$first"
}

# artefact_currency_scope_phrase
#
# The build-input scope as prose, DERIVED from `SHARED_TARGET_DIRT_SCOPES` and
# never typed. It exists so the sentence a reader sees and the pathspec the diff
# uses cannot drift: the messages that used to say "under native/rust" said it
# because a human wrote it there, and one of them stayed right only because
# the scope had not been widened yet. Prints `an UNDECLARED scope` when the array
# is missing, which is the honest phrase for the case the verdict refuses on.
artefact_currency_scope_phrase() {
  local sc out=""
  if [ -z "${SHARED_TARGET_DIRT_SCOPES+x}" ] || [ "${#SHARED_TARGET_DIRT_SCOPES[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
    printf 'an UNDECLARED scope'
    return 0
  fi
  for sc in "${SHARED_TARGET_DIRT_SCOPES[@]}"; do
    out="$out, ${sc#:(top)}"
  done
  printf '%s' "${out#, }"
}

# _build_inputs_changed <repo> <base>
#
# Prints the count of non-test files under the BUILD-INPUT SCOPE that the
# COMMITTED range <base>..HEAD touches. Prints `unknown` when git cannot answer,
# and `noscope` when the scope array is absent -- which is a REFUSAL and never a
# fallback to the old narrow pathspec, because an empty pathspec means the WHOLE
# repo and a defaulted one silently restores the defect this rename records.
_build_inputs_changed() {
  local repo="$1" base="$2" changed kept
  if [ -z "${SHARED_TARGET_DIRT_SCOPES+x}" ] || [ "${#SHARED_TARGET_DIRT_SCOPES[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
    printf 'noscope'; return 0
  fi
  $GIT_ISOLATED -C "$repo" rev-parse --git-dir >/dev/null 2>&1 || { printf 'unknown'; return 0; }
  $GIT_ISOLATED -C "$repo" cat-file -e "${base}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null || { printf 'unreachable'; return 0; }
  $GIT_ISOLATED -C "$repo" merge-base --is-ancestor "$base" HEAD 2>/dev/null || { printf 'notancestor'; return 0; }
  # NOT `|| true`: its three neighbours above each turn a failure into a NAMED
  # sentinel, and this line used to break that pattern. A swallowed failure left
  # `changed` empty, the count 0 and the verdict `ok` -- THE GUARD FAILING OPEN,
  # which is the one direction an ACTOR on PATH must never fail.
  if ! changed="$($GIT_ISOLATED -C "$repo" diff --name-only "$base..HEAD" -- "${SHARED_TARGET_DIRT_SCOPES[@]}" 2>/dev/null)"; then
    printf 'nodiff'
    return 0
  fi

  # COUNT WITHOUT LETTING `grep`'s NO-MATCH BECOME THIS FUNCTION'S EXIT STATUS.
  # A range touching ONLY `tests/` or `benches/` is the documented `ok` case,
  # and there `grep -v` selects nothing and exits 1. As the last command of the
  # function that rc IS the function's rc, so a caller running under `set -e`
  # WITHOUT wrapping the call in `$( )` dies on the healthiest possible answer.
  # The one caller, `artefact_currency_verdict`, survives only because a command
  # substitution DISARMS errexit in the subshell (`$-` measured: `ehuBc`
  # outside, `huBc` inside) -- ACCIDENTAL PROTECTION, not design, and the header
  # above promises that a library cannot kill its host. Found by critic-shell;
  # the consequence it predicted did not reproduce, and the mechanism under it
  # was real.
  kept="$(printf '%s\n' "$changed" | grep -vE '(^|/)(tests|benches)/' || true)"
  printf '%s\n' "$kept" | awk 'NF' | wc -l | tr -d ' '
}

# artefact_currency_verdict <release_dir> <repo>
#
# Prints exactly one of `ok`, `warn:<reason>`, `refuse:<reason>` and returns 0.
# The CALLER decides what a verdict costs -- this function never exits, because a
# library that kills its host cannot be used by a reporter and an actor both.
artefact_currency_verdict() {
  local dir="$1" repo="$2" marker base dirty=0 n
  if ! marker="$(artefact_pair_marker "$dir" 2>&1)"; then
    printf 'refuse:%s' "$marker"
    return 0
  fi
  base="$marker"
  case "$marker" in
    dirty-*) dirty=1; base="${marker#dirty-}" ;;
  esac

  n="$(_build_inputs_changed "$repo" "$base")"
  case "$n" in
    noscope)
      printf 'refuse:the build-input scope is undeclared -- SHARED_TARGET_DIRT_SCOPES is unset or empty, so currency cannot be decided. This is a REFUSAL rather than a narrower default: an empty pathspec means the whole repository and a defaulted one silently restores the single-path comparand this check was widened away from'
      return 0 ;;
    unknown)
      printf 'warn:cannot reach git, so currency is undecidable here -- the artefact is not at fault'
      return 0 ;;
    unreachable)
      printf 'refuse:the binaries name %s, which is not a commit in this repository' "${base:0:12}"
      return 0 ;;
    notancestor)
      printf 'refuse:the binaries name %s, which is not an ancestor of HEAD -- they describe a tree you are not standing on' "${base:0:12}"
      return 0 ;;
    nodiff)
      printf 'refuse:git is present and reachable but the range query %s..HEAD failed, so currency cannot be decided -- an actor refuses rather than execing on an undecided answer' "${base:0:12}"
      return 0 ;;
  esac

  # A COUNT OVER A `dirty-` BASE IS A FLOOR, NOT A DISTANCE (ic, 2026-08-24). The
  # committed range is enough to conclude STALE -- one changed source file does
  # that, and it needs none of the dirty argument. It is NOT enough to say HOW FAR
  # behind: whatever was uncommitted at build time lies outside the range, in
  # either direction. Saying "N files changed since" over a dirty base invites the
  # number to be quoted as the size of the gap, which is the overclaim this file
  # exists to refuse -- arriving in the file's own error message.
  if [ "$n" -gt 0 ]; then
    if [ "$dirty" -eq 1 ]; then
      printf 'refuse:at least %s non-test file(s) under %s changed since %s, and the marker is dirty- so that count is a FLOOR rather than the gap -- whatever was uncommitted at build time is outside this range. Behind HEAD either way' \
        "$n" "$(artefact_currency_scope_phrase)" "${base:0:12}"
    else
      printf 'refuse:%s non-test file(s) under %s changed since %s -- this binary is behind HEAD' \
        "$n" "$(artefact_currency_scope_phrase)" "${base:0:12}"
    fi
    return 0
  fi
  if [ "$dirty" -eq 1 ]; then
    printf 'warn:built from an UNCOMMITTED tree at %s; the committed range is source-clean, but what was uncommitted at build time is unrecoverable and this warning can never be cleared' \
      "${base:0:12}"
    return 0
  fi
  printf 'ok'
}
